# Saved progress

The current user request defines the task and its sequence in the conversation: plan first, delegate each task to its own subagent, and make no code edits as the coordinator. Requirements are defined by [the project map](../../../docs/README.md), especially [controls](../../../docs/interface/controls.md), [formats](../../../docs/sharing/reproducibility.md), and [architecture](../../../docs/technology/architecture.md).

Planning and required reads are complete. Core format and state work is done. Next: implement UI/session integration, then add browser regression coverage and run full validation. Only one subagent runs alongside the coordinator because the workspace has two slots.

Observed: snapshot validation required `version` to be the first JSON property, despite the format requiring exact keys without property order. Core now accepts reordered version 1 and 2 objects while enforcing exact version-specific keys. `GameState.name` defaults to empty; export chooses version 1 for empty and version 2 for named progress; imported version 1 clears it. `deal-name.ts` exports a 40 UTF-16-code-unit validator for the UI task. The focused snapshot suites pass (37 tests), all TypeScript projects typecheck, and modified core files pass Prettier. No product decision remains open.

Working files include `src/game/game.ts`, snapshot modules and tests, reproduction controls, session integration, application browser tests, and the project README. Each subagent must resolve context for its exact files, preserve unrelated changes, and report changes and test evidence. Git reads require `git -c safe.directory=/work` because of repository ownership.

